<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/15th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Regreg War</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1404">1404</a>–<a href="/years/1406">1406</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Regreg War was a civil war in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/majapahit-empire">Majapahit</a> kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a>, fought from 1404 to 1406 between the western court of King Wikramawardhana and the eastern court of Bhre Wirabhumi, a son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hayam-wuruk">Hayam Wuruk</a>. Wirabhumi was defeated and killed, but the war weakened Majapahit's hold over its overseas dependencies, and envoys of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zheng-he">Zheng He</a>'s Chinese fleet were killed in the fighting.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>An eastern realm had existed within <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/majapahit-empire">Majapahit</a> from its beginning: in 1295 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raden-wijaya">Raden Wijaya</a> gave the eastern part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-java">East Java</a>, with Lumajang as its capital, to Aria Wiraraja as a reward for his help. Ming Chinese chronicles record that by 1377 two kingdoms of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a> were sending envoys, the western under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hayam-wuruk">Hayam Wuruk</a> and the eastern under Bhre Wengker, also called Wijayarajasa, the husband of Hayam Wuruk's aunt, who had built a new eastern court at Pamotan.</p><p>When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hayam-wuruk">Hayam Wuruk</a> died in 1389 he was succeeded by his nephew and son-in-law Wikramawardhana, and when Wijayarajasa died in 1398 the eastern court passed to Bhre Wirabhumi, his foster son and husband of his granddaughter. A quarrel over the title of Bhre Lasem, which each court gave to a woman of its own, deepened the hostility, and in 1402, according to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pararaton">Pararaton</a>, the two rulers quarrelled bitterly and stopped speaking. In 1403 Wirabhumi sought Chinese help and accepted a seal and other insignia of Chinese suzerainty in return for recognition of his independence.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pararaton">Pararaton</a> names the conflict with the word regreg, meaning to proceed slowly or with halts and jerks; the usual name Paregreg comes from a misreading of the chronicle's prefix pa-, which simply marks an event. The name suggests many battles over the two years, won now by one court and now by the other. In 1406 western troops led by Bhre Tumapel, son of Wikramawardhana, broke into the eastern palace. Wirabhumi fled by boat at night but was caught and killed by Raden Gajah, who carried his head to the western court at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trowulan">Trowulan</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The two courts were reunited, but the long conflict had drained <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/majapahit-empire">Majapahit</a>, and its overseas <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a> stopped paying tribute; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palembang">Palembang</a>, Malayu and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malacca">Malacca</a> rebelled, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brunei">Brunei</a> threw off Javanese overlordship. About 170 Chinese envoys were killed when they were caught in the fighting, and the Ming court fined Wikramawardhana 60,000 tahil of gold; by 1408 he had paid only 10,000, and the emperor Yong Le in the end pardoned the rest. Wikramawardhana took Wirabhumi's daughter as a concubine, and their daughter <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suhita">Suhita</a>, queen from 1427, had Raden Gajah put to death in 1433. The war lived on in later Javanese literature as the tale of Damarwulan and Menak Jingga.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Regreg War” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>A civil war between the western and eastern courts, the <b>Regreg War</b> of 1404–1406, drained the kingdom's strength.</blockquote><a href="/empire/majapahit-empire">Majapahit Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>After Hayam Wuruk died in 1389, his son-in-law and his son by a concubine fought over it in the <b>Regreg War</b> of 1405–1406.</blockquote><a href="/societies/majapahit">Social Hierarchy of Majapahit</a></li></ul></section>
